==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{compound|en|soft|wood}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|mostly|botany}} The [[wood]] from any [[gymnosperm]], without regard to how [[soft]] this wood is.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1952|month=January|title=British Railways Standard Wagons|journal=Railway Magazine|page=61|text=The bodies are sheeted in '''softwood''', a double layer of ⅝-in. boards on the quarters, with a single thickness lining inside the steel ends.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|in more general use}} Wood of this kind, but limited to those that are [[commercial]] [[timber]]s.
# {{lb|en|countable|forestry}} The [[tree]] or tree [[species]] that [[yield]]s this wood.
#: {{ux|en|[[SYP]] is a '''softwood''', but it is harder than many hardwoods.}}
#: {{ux|en|This '''softwood''' has been planted extensively throughout Scotland.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Any commercial timber.
#: {{ux|en|You should have used '''softwood''' for the frame of this shed, instead of overbuilding it like this.}}

